 "verdict": "RUNG (AR backend): the paired path under monotone reproduces ar_probe.block_damage to 0.0 in the cone, against a production mean damage of 0.5177408854166667 -> PASS. CURVES: step256 monotone +0.0083 vs maximal -0.0303; step512 monotone +0.0666 vs maximal -0.0203; step143000 monotone +0.1824 vs maximal +0.1469. CROSSING TEST: NOT INFORMATIVE at this geometry. lambda(step256) is +0.0083 +/- 0.0440 under monotone and -0.0303 +/- 0.0355 under maximal -- neither is distinguishable from zero, so whether the SIGN changes is not determined at 8 seeds and cannot separate the couplings. Reported rather than quietly dropped: it was the registered primary's first leg. OFFSET: maximal reads lower at every checkpoint, by -0.0385, -0.0869, -0.0355 -- the direction F41 predicts, since maximal coupling maximises agreement and so minimises damage. The offsets span 0.0514 against a floor of 0.0358, so they are uniform within noise. COMMON MODE, for the comparison the paper actually makes: the ORDERING of the three checkpoints is identical under both couplings, and the offset between them is uniform within the seed floor. Rows 3 and 4 of the discriminator table read the model, not the coupling. F41's escape clause holds here -- measured, where it had been asserted. Gates: dynamic_range: lambda across the three checkpoints (monotone) spans 0.1741 against its own noise floor 0.03579 (4.86x, gate 2.0x); distinct_units: 3 sampled checkpoint collapse to 3 distinct (gate 3) BOUNDARY: one geometry (r=2, T=0.7, N=48, B=16), one family, three checkpoints. A pass licenses THESE comparisons, not coupling-invariance in general. F41's absolute gap ({'T0.7': 1.013, 'T0.9': 1.054}) is untouched -- this is about the relative reading only.",
